 is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535401 true] [17592186088177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535401 true] [17592186088177 1100 "Consider the equation $y^{″} + 12y^{′} + 40y = 0$, where $y = y(x)$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution, then $λ =$" 13194139535401 false] [17592186088177 1100 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139535401 true] [17592186088177 1145 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40=0\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535401 true] [17592186088295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535401 true] [17592186088295 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$" 13194139535401 true] [17592186088295 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 16y = 0$." 13194139535401 false] [17592186088295 1145 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+16=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535401 true] [17592186088297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535401 true] [17592186088297 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+26y=0.\n$$" 13194139535401 true] [17592186088297 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + 26y = 0$." 13194139535401 false] [17592186088297 1145 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+26{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+